solution_str:
First, let's determine how much money Kathy will withdraw in each currency. Since she has $300 in total and wants to withdraw half of it in pounds and the other half in euros, she will withdraw:

- \dfrac{300}{2} = 150 \text{ USD worth of pounds}
- \dfrac{300}{2} = 150 \text{ USD worth of euros}

Now, we need to convert these amounts into the respective currencies' units:

- 1 pound = 1.64 USD, so 150 USD is equivalent to \dfrac{150}{1.64} pounds.
- 1 euro = 1.32 USD, so 150 USD is equivalent to \dfrac{150}{1.32} euros.

Next, we calculate the number of pounds and euros:

\text{Pounds} = \dfrac{150}{1.64} \approx 91.51 \text{ pounds}
\text{Euros} = \dfrac{150}{1.32} \approx 113.64 \text{ euros}

The difference between the number of euros and the number of pounds is:

\text{Difference} = 114 - 92 = 22

Rounding to the nearest whole number, we get:

\boxed{22}

